<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1881 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hotaru no Hikari</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">蛍の光</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Hotaru no Hikari</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese school song of 1881 sung to the tune of Auld Lang Syne at graduations and at the end of the year.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Chikai Inagaki</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the Scottish tune of Auld Lang Syne)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1881 (Meiji 14)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Inagaki Chikai (1881))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a>&quot; (&quot;Glow of a firefly&quot;) is a Japanese song that sets new and entirely different words by Chikai Inagaki to the tune of the Scottish folk song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a>&quot;. It was first published in 1881 (Meiji 14) in a collection of songs for elementary school pupils. Putting new words to an existing tune without changing the music much is known as contrafactum.</p><p>The words draw on the story of Che Yin in volume 83 of the Book of Jin, about a student who kept studying by the light of fireflies when he had no other light.</p><p>The song is commonly heard at graduation ceremonies and at the end of the school day, and many shops and restaurants play it to tell customers that they are closing for the day. It has become a tradition for all the performers to sing it as the last song of NHK's New Year's Eve programme Kōhaku Uta Gassen, and from 1993 until 2020 it was played at Tokyo Disney Resort during the New Year's Eve fireworks. &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aogeba-totoshi">Aogeba Tōtoshi</a>&quot;, another graduation song of the same period, is also thought to be based on a Scottish folk song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song follows a hard-working student through a series of images of the hardships he endures in his pursuit of learning, beginning with the firefly's light by which he reads. Its mood is earnest and, in its use at graduations and closings, one of parting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ほたるのひかり。まどのゆき。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Hotaru no hikari, mado no yuki,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The light of the fireflies, the snow at the window:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">書（ふみ）よむつき日。かさねつゝ。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">fumi yomu tsukihi, kasanetsutsu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the days and months of reading books we have piled up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">いつしか年も。すぎのとを。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">itsushika toshi mo, sugi no to o</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and before we knew it the years too have passed; the cedar door</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">あけてぞけさは。わかれゆく。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">akete zo kesa wa, wakare yuku.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we open, and this morning we part and go.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">とまるもゆくも。かぎりとて。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Tomaru mo yuku mo, kagiri tote,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those who stay and those who go, at this the end,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">かたみにおもふちよろづの。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">katami ni omou chiyorozu no</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the ten thousand thousand things each thinks for the other,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">こゝろのはしを。ひとことに。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kokoro no hashi o, hitokoto ni,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the edge of our hearts, in a single word,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">さきくとばかり。うたふなり。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">sakiku to bakari, utau nari.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>only 'be well!' is what we sing.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">つくしのきはみ。みちのおく。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Tsukushi no kiwami, Michi no oku,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The far end of Tsukushi, the depths of Michinoku,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">うみやまとほく。へだつとも。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">umi yama tōku, hedatsu tomo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though sea and mountain lie far between us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">そのまごころは。へだてなく。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">sono magokoro wa, hedate naku,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that true heart, with nothing between,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ひとつにつくせ。くにのため。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">hitotsu ni tsukuse, kuni no tame.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give it whole, as one, for the country.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">千島（ちしま）のおくも。おきなはも。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Chishima no oku mo, Okinawa mo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The far reaches of Chishima, and Okinawa too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">やしまのうちの。まもりなり。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Yashima no uchi no, mamori nari.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are the guard of what lies within the Eight Islands.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">いたらんくにに。いさをしく。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Itaran kuni ni, isaoshiku</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the land you go to, valiantly</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">つとめよわがせ。つつがなく。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tsutomeyo waga se, tsutsuga naku.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do your duty, my dear one, and come to no harm.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Inagaki Chikai; Shōgaku shōka-shū, first volume (1881). Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%9B%8D%E3%81%AE%E5%85%89_(%E5%B0%8F%E5%AD%A6%E5%94%B1%E6%AD%8C%E9%9B%86%E5%88%9D%E7%B7%A8)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: modified Hepburn of the words as they are read and sung; the historical kana spelling of the original is not transliterated letter by letter (おもふ omou, うたふ utau, きはみ kiwami, とほく tōku, おきなは Okinawa, いさをしく isaoshiku). Letter by letter the written kana would give omofu, utafu, kihami, tohoku, Okinaha, isawoshiku.</li><li>The original separates phrases with 。; the romanization gives them as commas inside a line and a full stop where the sense ends. Glosses in brackets in the original (書（ふみ）, 千島（ちしま）) are readings and are romanized once (fumi, Chishima); ゝ (repeat sign) is written out (kasanetsutsu, kokoro).</li><li>line 1.1: fireflies and window snow allude to the Chinese story of two poor scholars who read by the light of fireflies and by the light of snow; the line names the images only, as the original does.</li><li>lines 1.3-1.4: 'sugi' is a pivot word: 'passing' (the years have passed) and 'cedar' (sugi no to, 'the cedar door'); 'akete' is both 'opening' the door and the day or year breaking. The translation keeps the door; the double sense cannot be kept in English.</li><li>line 2.2: 'katami ni' = 'mutually, each for the other'; 'chiyorozu' (a thousand times ten thousand) = countless.</li><li>line 2.4: 'sakiku' = 'safely, in good fortune' (an archaic farewell word), given as 'be well!'.</li><li>line 3.1: Tsukushi = the old name of Kyushu (the far south-west); Michi no oku (Michinoku) = the far north of Honshu. Kept as names.</li><li>line 4.1: Chishima = the Kuril Islands; Okinawa = the Ryukyus: the northern and southern limits of the realm in 1881. This stanza was rewritten in later printings as the borders changed; the text here is the 1881 one.</li><li>line 4.2: Yashima, 'the Eight Islands', an old name for Japan.</li><li>line 4.4: 'waga se', 'my se': an old word a woman uses for her husband or brother (or a beloved man); rendered 'my dear one'. The stanza reads as a farewell to a man going to guard the frontier.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
